  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nTuesday...\n\nRRFS and HRRR both have a signal for additional smoke concentrations \nback behind the leading cold front tonight, but there is a muted \nsignal for any visibility reductions. Outside of this, a \nstrengthening upper jet and colder temperatures aloft will promote \ncumulus development during the afternoon. A secondary and more \nnoteworthy front will sweep southward across the Great Lakes during \nthe afternoon hours possibly accompanied by low-topped showers and \nsprinkles, but not before temperatures recover back to the low to \nmid 80s. Northwesterly winds will increase and gust in the 20 to 25 \nmph range during the afternoon. The surface high, quite strong for \nmid July, will strengthen further Tuesday night and dip south across \nthe upper Midwest. A sharp upper trough will pass over the Great \nLakes and push a notably cooler and drier airmass into the lower \nGreat Lakes region overnight into Wednesday. There is a limited QPF \nfootprint on several guidance sources, and cloud depths may get just \ntall enough for some sprinkles or low topped showers later in the \nday into the evening, highest confidence into NW Indiana. This will \nset the stage for some seasonably comfortable overnight lows (in the \n50s) for many areas outside the urban core. \n\nKMD\n\nWednesday through Sunday...\n\nWednesday onward, deep cyclonic upper-level flow will only gradually \nshift eastward as a surface high pressure system meanders through \nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley. The net result should be a \nperiod of pleasant conditions with no precipitation, highs in the \nupper 70s to around 80, and overnight lows in the upper 50s to \naround 60. Choppy waves will make swimming hazardous on Lake \nMichigan beaches Wednesday and perhaps into early Thursday. \n\nEnsemble guidance depicts the redevelopment of an expansive upper-\nlevel ridge across the central United States toward next weekend \nwhich would encourage temperatures locally to climb back into the \nupper 80s to perhaps lower 90s. There is one ripple in the monsoonal \nmoisture that should be able to progress toward the Ohio Valley on \nFriday, but most guidance sends any precipitation with this wave \nwell south of the local area.  There is still a signal for a \nconvective event sometime in the July 27-29 timeframe in the general \nregion with northwest flow in place on the northeast fringes of the \nstrong upper ridge. This would pose the next chance of meaningful \nrainfall should this system move toward our latitude. \n\nBorchardt/KMD",
